{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
Be[G]lieve me, if all those
En[D7]dearing young [G]charms,
Which I [G]gaze on so [C]fondly to[D7]day,
Were to [G]change by to-morrow,
And [D7]fleet in my [G]arms,
Like [C]fairy-gifts [G]fading a[D7]way,
Thou wouldst [G]still be adored,
As this [D7]moment thou [G]art,
Let thy [C]loveliness [G]fade as it [D7]will,
And a[G]round the dear ruin
Each [D7]wish of my [G]heart
Would en[C]twine itself [G]verdant[D7]ly [G]still.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
It [G]is not while beauty
And [D7]youth are thine [G]own,
And thy [G]cheeks unpro[C]faned by a [D7]tear,
That the [G]fervour and faith
Of a [D7]soul can be [G]known,
To which [C]time will but [G]make thee more [D7]dear;
No, the [G]heart that has truly loved
[D7]Never for[G]gets,
But as [C]truly loves [G]on to the [D7]close,
As the [G]sunflower turns on her
[D7]God, when he [G]sets,
The same [C]look which she [G]turned when [D7]he [G]rose.
{end_of_verse}
{x_end: G}
